On Sunday, April 8, 2012 8:32:53 PM UTC-4, Jonadab the Unsightly One wrote:

> One last time, the problems as I see them:
> 
>  A. Not many major goals you can work on.
>  B. Lots of levels that take a long time to traverse EVERY TIME.
>  C. Lots of nearly-identical levels that take a long time to explore.

To be fair, I consider these three problems very closely related, and
connected to one of the main plot points of NetHack.  When you enter
the Dungeons of Doom, your ability is limited to surviving -- if you're
lucky; most characters can't even achieve that.  By the time you reach
Astral, of course, the other Riders recognize you as a peer, and when 
you leave it, you're generally a demigod, with whatever demi-omnipotence
that suggests.  Gehennom comes at the halfway point between those two 
mileposts, and your abilities (and aspirations!) shouldn't be limited
to surviving and traversing dungeon levels at that point.

The challenge presented by the labyrinth mazes in Gehennom is 
redesigning them.  Using pickaxe, dig spells, wands, or (my favorite) 
tame umber hulks, the obvious task is to dig out a straight path 
between stairs; the overachievers or overpreparers prefer to dig the
mazes out entirely.  When rebuilding Gehennom mazes, you become in a
sense the lord of the dungeon -- the guy who is controlling the 
environment to meet his own needs, to facilitate destroying his foes.
It's the sort of behavior we normally attribute to the Wizard, whom 
you supplant in other ways (taking his relics, achieving his
ambition, destroying his minions) before rising above him and 
leaving him a distant memory just outside the Dungeon entrance.

The lairs are really the frustrating ones in my opinion -- they don't
allow you to prepare them for the ascension run, to soften the effect
of that Mysterious Force.  Either they should be somehow taken out of
the Mysterious Force range (maybe make them the bottom levels, right
above the VS, and don't let the MF kick in till above them?) or they
should be made diggable and teleportable.  I decline to believe that
Demogorgon is suddenly an easy fight if I can dig through walls and
teleport on his level, or if I take away his stair retreat (and I 
decline to believe that because I -always- lure demon lords to the 
next lower level when I fight them).  The one-lair-only variant 
helps that a lot.

>  D. Annoying enemies.  Glob flies, Worf would call them.
>  E. Not enough intesting things to find.
>  F. Not many interesting dangers to face.

The quests demonstrate that you can make levels that are geared to 
specific monster generation.  I'd love to see a Dragon level -- maybe
a maze level or even a branch -- with nothing but a million dragons 
of all hues within.  It'd be boring if you genocided them, but if 
you hadn't gotten the DSM you wanted or that disintegration 
resistance, worth having.  

I'd really like to see a few single-level branches off Gehennom that 
are geared toward single monsters and don't respect genocide.  Maybe
have a wand of wishing or a random high-quality artifact guaranteed 
on each level, but make them extra hard -- a level of demi- and 
arch-lichs, or a level of master and standard mindflayers.  A whole 
level of Archons, Angels and Aleaxes. Guaranteed wands of wishing 
and quest artifacts qualify as E, I think, and those levels can 
count as F, as well.


